  "patches": [
    {
      "family": "relations",
      "grounding_index": 0,
      "reason": "The exact passage supports the specialised cumulative-input contract, its refinement scope, domain and precondition restrictions, and access beyond the immediately preceding output.",
      "record_key": "environment_carries_function_inputs",
      "replacement": {
        "end_line": 402,
        "quote": "lets a refinement carry an earlier output to a later step only through a\ndeclared adapter or specialised contract whose declared domain names it,\nand names [PROFILES §2](PROFILES.md#2-graph-execution), which indexes this\nprofile, as the keeper of the ODD binding. This profile declares the\ncumulative carried environment of\n[§11.8](#118-cumulative-environment-law-is-mandatory) as that specialised\ncontract; §11.8 is conserved as profile law and is not generalised into\nthe core. Direction: from the typed refinement of ACTIVATION §4 to a graph\nfunction that refines one outer vector, with the §11.8 environment as its\nspecialised contract. Preserved meaning: the outer vector stays the\ncallable contract; the inner steps conserve it and every applicable\ninvariant; and each inner function's input satisfies its declared domain\nand preconditions, as AC-016 requires. Narrowed scope: one published outer\nvector and its declared inner workflow, whose retained functions should\ndeclare `environment.requires`, `environment.provides` and\n`environment.carries` (§11.8); a later function's declared domain is its\n`environment.requires`, which may require any typed binding available in\nthe cumulative carried environment, not only the immediately previous\noutput.",
        "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F5.%20Graph%20model%2FGraph%20functions",
        "start_line": 384
      }
    },
    {
      "family": "constraints",
      "grounding_index": 0,
      "reason": "Corrects the transcribed section identity and quotes the supplied manual-conformance and negative-proof rules. The unchanged repetition claim is also supported by lines 134 and 164 of this section.",
      "record_key": "manual_conformance_and_negative_proof",
      "replacement": {
        "end_line": 138,
        "quote": "| P0 (recovered), P6 (independent); I3; F13 (imported) | automation decides nothing that the declared surfaces do not: each automated move is one an actor holding only those surfaces could make, because a later actor re-acquires declared sources and not private memory, and authority comes only from declared owners; an agentic worker's artifacts take effect only through admission against deterministic criteria, and conformance evidence is not ratification | [§11.7](#117-manual-walkthrough-must-still-be-lawful); [§11.7A](#117a-conforming-agentic-development) | the manual walkthrough as the test of automation, and its agentic form as the conformance proof of the method: this member; the admission criteria for produced artifacts: [SPEC_METHOD](SPEC_METHOD.md) and [TICKETS](TICKETS.md) | 5, 9, 7 |\n| P6 (independent), P5 (derived from P0 and P6); F6 (imported) | By P6 only the real authority-bearing input decides, so negative evidence for a boundary exercises the supported authority path with that input invalidated and shows refusal before the prohibited effect (F6); by P5 a manufactured refusal, blocked event, gap or verdict proves nothing about that path, and absence in source, package, installation and runtime are distinct claims | [§11.7B](#117b-real-authority-path-negative-proof-stdo-up-010) | the concrete test and evidence mechanism: the consumer | 9, 5 |\n",
        "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F1.%20Position%2FFoundation%20to%20law",
        "start_line": 137
      }
    }
